<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.4: Identifying Graph Regions Included in a System's Solution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00644</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.4: Identifying Graph Regions Included in a System's Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4d8fc66f-8a14-4ad5-ba7f-0b724d242e58</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Use this information to answer questions 1–7.
</para><blockquote>
<para>A factory produces “widgets” and “zurls.” The combined number of widgets and zurls made each day cannot be more than 12. The maximum number of widgets the factory can produce in a day is 4.</para>
<para>Let \(x\) be the number of widgets and \(y\) be the number of zurls.</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>Select <emphasis>two</emphasis> inequalities that represent this situation. 
        <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x\leq4\) and \(x+y\leq12\)</para>
    </item>   
    <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equations \(x=4\) and \(x+y=12\). (Students were provided access to Desmos.)  
        <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> </para>
       <figure><media alt="Graph of a line, origin O. X axis from 0 to 12 by 2’s. Y axis from 0 to 12 by 2’s. Line passes through 0 comma 12, 2 comma 10, 4 comma 8, 6 comma 6, 8 comma 4, 10 comma 2 and 12 comma 0.  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c139281acbcf5a56cdc0953109560629168ca9a6"/></media></figure></item> 
       
       <item>After graphing the equations \(x=4\) and \(x+y=12\), shade the regions and adjust the line types as needed to show all the allowable numbers of widgets and zurls that the factory can produce in one day. (Students were provided access to Desmos.) 
       <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
    <figure><media alt="Two inequalities on a coordinate plane  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a20ed44fb6a7d9bcc05e1875ebb23da25d3b157c"/></media></figure></item> 
</list>
<para>In questions 4–7 answer the question: </para><blockquote>Does the ordered pair represent an allowable combination of widgets and zurls produced in one day?</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item>(4, 5)
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes</para>
    </item>
    <item>(11, 1)
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No</para>
    </item>
    <item>(4, 12)
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No</para>
    </item>
    <item>(3, 9)
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes</para>
    </item>

</list>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>If students are still struggling to shade graphs appropriately, highlight student work from the cool-down to address misconceptions and assign practice problems from this lesson for additional opportunities for students to practice</para>
 </section>
</content>
</document>
